☆JPA - First level of caching
   In first level of caching 1 new entity manager will be created to each of the http request and entiryManager will create PersistentContext for each request.
 When read call will be made JPA will try to find the data in Persistent context first if not found it'll do the select call to DB and put it in the PersistentContext any subsequent calls made in the same httpRequest will fetch the data from same PersistentContext

☆Level two caching
  - To enable 2nd level caching we basically need 3 dependencies(any cache provider-> Ex: ehcache, interface to interact with from  jcache -> cache-api, interface to interact between hibernate and jcache-api -> hibernate-jcache)
  - once dependecies added we can enable caching by setting enable second level caching set to true, also we need to set the region factory class in app.properies(which helps grouping entity to region so that we can set caching stratergies to a region the configs such as cache time etc...), Additionally we need to provide ehcachce config in app.properties.
  - To mark an entity as cached we need to add @cache annotation which accepts 2 values usage( cacheconcurrncy stratergy), region
  - CacheConcurrencuStratreiges-> READ_ONLY(only enables reading on the entity no updates are allowed) READ_WRITE(It puts exclusive lock on the cache entity and mark with inavalidate flag then updates thr data on DB after it'll update the cache aswell), NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE(it'll mark the entity with invalidate flag in cache and update date in db but, no update of cache will happen any get call will hit the db directly when flag is true), TRANSACTIONAL(it aquires lock for both read and write operation any read or write call during lock will go to db directly)
